# Concatenated Words

**LeetCode Problem:** [472. Concatenated Words](https://leetcode.com/problems/concatenated-words/)

## Approach: Trie + DFS + Memoization

- First, insert all the given words into a Trie, where each node maintains its children and whether it represents the end of a word.
- For every word, use DFS to check whether it can be segmented into smaller dictionary words.
- Start traversing the Trie from the current position in the word.
- Whenever a complete word is found, recursively check the remaining suffix by restarting Trie traversal from the root.
- Maintain a count of the number of words used to form the current word. A word is considered concatenated only if it consists of at least two smaller words.
- Use memoization to avoid recalculating previously explored positions and reduce redundant recursive calls.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    struct trieNode {
        bool isEndOfWord;
        trieNode* children[26];
    };

    trieNode* getNode() {
        trieNode* newNode = new trieNode();
        newNode->isEndOfWord = false;

        for(int i = 0; i < 26; i++)
            newNode->children[i] = NULL;

        return newNode;
    }

    bool solve(trieNode* root, string& word, int start,
               int count, vector<vector<int>>& dp) {

        if(start == word.length())
            return count >= 2;

        if(dp[start][min(count, 2)] != -1)
            return dp[start][min(count, 2)];

        trieNode* node = root;

        for(int i = start; i < word.length(); i++) {
            int idx = word[i] - 'a';

            if(node->children[idx] == NULL)
                break;

            node = node->children[idx];

            if(node->isEndOfWord) {
                if(solve(root, word, i + 1, count + 1, dp))
                    return dp[start][min(count, 2)] = 1;
            }
        }

        return dp[start][min(count, 2)] = 0;
    }

    vector<string> findAllConcatenatedWordsInADict(
        vector<string>& words) {

        trieNode* root = getNode();

        for(const string& s : words) {
            trieNode* node = root;

            for(char ch : s) {
                int idx = ch - 'a';

                if(node->children[idx] == NULL)
                    node->children[idx] = getNode();

                node = node->children[idx];
            }

            node->isEndOfWord = true;
        }

        vector<string> ans;

        for(string& s : words) {
            vector<vector<int>> dp(s.length(), vector<int>(3, -1));

            if(solve(root, s, 0, 0, dp))
                ans.push_back(s);
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

Let:
- \(W\) = Number of words.
- \(L\) = Maximum length of a word.

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(W \cdot L^2)\) — Trie construction takes \(O(W \cdot L)\), and checking each word using DFS and memoization takes \(O(L^2)\).
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(W \cdot L)\) — For storing the Trie and the auxiliary DP and recursion space.
